# Lee’s Market — FSMA 204 requirements matrix (M1 research)

**Prepared for:** M3, Regulatory Affairs Strategy and Report Lead  
**Reference date in task:** 2025-08-27. FDA pages were also checked as currently posted on 2026-11-20; timing is separated below because the status changed after the report date.

## Executive summary

FSMA 204 is a product-and-activity-based rule. Lee’s Market should map each U.S.-bound product against FDA’s Food Traceability List (FTL), then map each facility’s activities to the applicable Critical Tracking Events (CTEs). For a covered retail food establishment (RFE), the most likely routine obligation is to preserve receiving data for listed foods. Distribution to another business or processing a listed food can create additional shipping or transformation duties. [FDA final rule](https://www.fda.gov/food/food-safety-modernization-act-fsma/fsma-final-rule-requirements-additional-traceability-records-certain-foods) · [FDA FAQ](https://www.fda.gov/food/food-safety-modernization-act-fsma/frequently-asked-questions-fsma-food-traceability-rule)

## Requirements matrix

| Topic | FDA requirement / Lee’s Market relevance |
|---|---|
| **Who and what is covered** | A person who manufactures, processes, packs, or holds food on the FTL generally must keep the additional traceability records for the CTEs it performs, unless an exemption applies. FDA describes the rule as applying to domestic and foreign entities; U.S.-bound foods handled by Lee’s Canadian and U.S. operations therefore need a U.S. jurisdiction review. FDA-regulated foods are in scope; foods under exclusive USDA jurisdiction are generally outside this rule. The existing FDA/CFIA, HACCP, or USDA compliance credentials do not by themselves establish that the FTL records are complete. [FDA final rule](https://www.fda.gov/food/food-safety-modernization-act-fsma/fsma-final-rule-requirements-additional-traceability-records-certain-foods) · [21 CFR Part 1, Subpart S](https://www.ecfr.gov/current/title-21/chapter-I/subchapter-A/part-1/subpart-S) |
| **FTL products likely in Lee’s assortment** | FDA lists: cheeses other than hard cheeses; shell eggs; nut butters; fresh cucumbers; fresh-cut fruits; fresh-cut vegetables other than leafy greens; fresh herbs; leafy greens (fresh and fresh-cut); melons; fresh peppers; sprouts; fresh tomatoes; tropical tree fruits; finfish; crustaceans; molluscan shellfish; and ready-to-eat deli salads. Prioritize Asian produce (e.g., leafy greens, herbs, cucumber, pepper, tomato, melon, tropical fruit, sprouts and cut produce), seafood, eggs, nut butters, qualifying cheeses and deli salads. Generic meat, bakery and prepared-food labels are not automatically covered: classify each SKU and ingredient/process against the FTL; USDA-regulated meat/poultry products and non-FTL bakery items generally are outside this specific rule. [FDA Food Traceability List](https://www.fda.gov/food/food-safety-modernization-act-fsma/food-traceability-list) · [FDA FAQ](https://www.fda.gov/food/food-safety-modernization-act-fsma/frequently-asked-questions-fsma-food-traceability-rule) |
| **CTEs and where they arise** | FDA’s CTEs include growing, harvesting, cooling, initial packing, first land-based receiving (seafood), shipping, receiving and transformation. Produce farms/suppliers commonly perform growing through initial packing; seafood suppliers may perform first land-based receiving; Lee’s stores/DCs commonly perform receiving and may perform shipping or transformation. Receiving a finished FTL food is a CTE. Cutting, mixing, repacking or making a listed food may trigger transformation requirements; whether a specific activity changes an item’s status depends on the product and FDA definitions. [FDA final rule](https://www.fda.gov/food/food-safety-modernization-act-fsma/fsma-final-rule-requirements-additional-traceability-records-certain-foods) · [FDA CTE/KDE overview](https://www.fda.gov/media/163014/download) |
| **Receiving KDEs (key data elements)** | For each covered receipt, retain the applicable product description, quantity and unit of measure, receiving date and location, traceability lot code (TLC), TLC source, immediate previous source (not merely the carrier), and reference-document type/number. These records let Lee’s connect the lot received to the upstream supply-chain record. The exact KDE set is event-specific; use FDA’s FAQ and regulation when configuring each workflow. [FDA FAQ](https://www.fda.gov/food/food-safety-modernization-act-fsma/frequently-asked-questions-fsma-food-traceability-rule) · [21 CFR § 1.1345](https://www.ecfr.gov/current/title-21/chapter-I/subchapter-A/part-1/subpart-S/subject-group-ECFRbfe98fb65ccc9f7/section-1.1345) |
| **Shipping KDEs** | Where Lee’s ships a covered food to another business (e.g., a store transfer or wholesale customer), shipping records generally identify the TLC, product and quantity, ship date, shipping and receiving locations, and a reference document. FDA provides a limited exception for shipping records for food sent directly to a consumer by an RFE/restaurant; do not extend that exception to business-to-business distribution without checking the exact facts. [FDA FAQ](https://www.fda.gov/food/food-safety-modernization-act-fsma/frequently-asked-questions-fsma-food-traceability-rule) · [21 CFR § 1.1340](https://www.ecfr.gov/current/title-21/chapter-I/subchapter-A/part-1/subpart-S/subject-group-ECFRbfe98fb65ccc9f7/section-1.1340) |
| **Transformation and TLC continuity** | A TLC links the food across events. FDA requires assignment at specified points (including initial packing, first land-based receiving for seafood, and transformation), rather than a new code at every handoff; subsequent handlers record and preserve the code and its source. A transformation record must link input lot(s) to the output lot/product and capture the applicable quantities, date, location and reference document. Lee’s should test store cutting/repacking, seafood preparation, deli preparation, and online order picking with FDA’s product-specific definitions before deciding which processes are covered. [FDA Traceability Lot Code page](https://www.fda.gov/food/food-safety-modernization-act-fsma/traceability-lot-code) · [21 CFR § 1.1350](https://www.ecfr.gov/current/title-21/chapter-I/subchapter-A/part-1/subpart-S/subject-group-ECFRbfe98fb65ccc9f7/section-1.1350) |
| **Record format, retention and FDA access** | Keep required records for two years. FDA may request the records and they must be provided within 24 hours or another reasonable time agreed to by FDA. FDA may also request an electronic sortable spreadsheet with the relevant traceability information, subject to specified exceptions. The rule does not require a particular commercial traceability platform; Lee’s can use existing systems if they capture, link, retain and retrieve the required KDEs. [FDA final rule](https://www.fda.gov/food/food-safety-modernization-act-fsma/fsma-final-rule-requirements-additional-traceability-records-certain-foods) · [FDA FAQ](https://www.fda.gov/food/food-safety-modernization-act-fsma/frequently-asked-questions-fsma-food-traceability-rule) |
| **Traceability plan** | Covered entities must maintain a traceability plan describing procedures for maintaining records, identifying foods on the FTL, assigning TLCs (if applicable), and identifying a contact for FDA. Farms have additional map requirements. For Lee’s, one controlled product/event map can standardize compliance across vendors, DCs, stores and e-commerce fulfillment. [FDA final rule](https://www.fda.gov/food/food-safety-modernization-act-fsma/fsma-final-rule-requirements-additional-traceability-records-certain-foods) · [21 CFR Part 1, Subpart S](https://www.ecfr.gov/current/title-21/chapter-I/subchapter-A/part-1/subpart-S) |
| **Exemptions / limits** | A small RFE/restaurant exemption exists when the average annual monetary value of food sold directly to consumers during the prior three years is at or below the inflation-adjusted statutory threshold (base amount $250,000). The indexed amount and the correct calculation/establishment basis should be confirmed before relying on it; Lee’s corporate revenue alone does not answer the facility-level question. The rule also has product-, activity- and entity-specific exemptions, and generally does not cover foods absent from the FTL or foods regulated exclusively by USDA. FDA’s FAQ discusses food containing an FTL ingredient and the effect of transformation; assess deli recipes and prepared foods individually. [FDA FAQ](https://www.fda.gov/food/food-safety-modernization-act-fsma/frequently-asked-questions-fsma-food-traceability-rule) · [21 CFR § 1.1305](https://www.ecfr.gov/current/title-21/chapter-I/subchapter-A/part-1/subpart-S/section-1.1305) |
| **Deadline / status** | **As of the task’s 2025-08-27 reference date:** the rule’s original compliance date was January 20, 2026. FDA had announced an intention to extend it in March 2025 and, on August 6, 2025, proposed a 30-month extension to July 20, 2028. The August action was a proposal, not a final amendment on the task date; report July 20, 2028 as proposed, not as an already-finalized statutory date. [FDA March 20, 2025 announcement](https://www.fda.gov/food/hfp-constituent-updates/fda-intends-extend-compliance-date-food-traceability-rule) · [FDA August 6, 2025 proposal](https://www.fda.gov/food/hfp-constituent-updates/fda-proposes-extend-compliance-date-food-traceability-rule-and-issues-new-faqs-and-other-resources) **Current FDA posting checked 2026-11-20:** FDA’s rule page still calls the 30-month change a proposal and notes a subsequent Congressional directive that FDA not enforce before July 20, 2028. Distinguish the rule’s formal compliance-date amendment from the current non-enforcement position, and recheck FDA before publication. [FDA current rule page](https://www.fda.gov/food/food-safety-modernization-act-fsma/fsma-final-rule-requirements-additional-traceability-records-certain-foods) |

## Questions for Lee’s counsel / regulatory confirmation

1. Which U.S. and Canadian legal entities, facilities and store/DC locations manufacture, process, pack or hold foods destined for U.S. consumption, and which are covered persons under the rule?
2. What are Lee’s actual SKUs/ingredients on the FTL, including produce state (whole vs. fresh-cut), seafood type, cheese type, deli salad recipes and imported foods? Resolve any mixed USDA/FDA jurisdiction products.
3. Does the small-RFE exemption apply to any individual Lee’s operation after applying the correct indexed threshold and FDA’s prior-three-year sales calculation? Do not infer the answer solely from chain-wide company revenue.
4. Which store/DC workflows count as transformation or business-to-business shipping (including trimming/cutting, repacking, seafood/deli preparation, store transfers and third-party fulfillment)? Which online/delivery transactions qualify for direct-to-consumer exceptions?
5. What data can existing ERP/POS/WMS and supplier records provide for TLC, TLC source, prior source, receiving date/location, units/quantity and reference documents? Can staff retrieve a complete lot trace within 24 hours?
6. Confirm current compliance/enforcement timing and any later FDA flexibility or exemption before final approval; the date has changed since the task’s 2025-08-27 reference date.

## Suggested report framing

Position FSMA 204 as a way to make recall scope and customer response faster and more precise: map listed SKUs, capture receiving KDEs consistently, preserve lot linkage through store/DC handoffs, run mock trace exercises, and communicate only what the records support. FDA describes faster identification and removal of potentially contaminated food as the rule’s purpose; the researched materials do not quantify Lee’s-specific customer-trust gains, cost savings or recall reductions. Avoid implying that compliance guarantees food is risk-free. [FDA final rule](https://www.fda.gov/food/food-safety-modernization-act-fsma/fsma-final-rule-requirements-additional-traceability-records-certain-foods)
